  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nUpper trough and induced low pressure system will traverse the Upper \nMidwest tonight, moving eastward with time on Wednesday into N MI. \nPressure gradient increases some with the relatively \"weak\" low \npressure system, and thus breezy conditions are anticipated through \nthe day. Main story will be the convective evolution and what that \nmight look like.\n\nWarm advection coupled with upper forcing/divergence within an \nenvironment characterized by sufficient moisture and instability \nwill result in scattered to numerous thunderstorms on Wednesday, \nbeginning west of 75 in the morning up into the E UP, then migrating \nand redeveloping eastward during the afternoon hours. Any stronger \nstorm will be capable of locally gusty winds and hail. Any severe \nthreat looks rather isolated and localized in nature, given the high \nmoisture content and modest shear (25 kts of bulk 0-6km, little more \n0-8km, although that can be enough to generate transiently strong \nstorms). Given trough orientation/forcing and instability axis, \nnortheast lower into eastern upper will \"likely\" see the best \nopportunity for locally heavy rainfall with training storms. That \nbeing said, the atmosphere will be primed with ~1.4\"-1.5\" PWATs, \nsignificant moistening in the low to mid levels, and seemingly more \nthan enough instability for any storm to produce locally heavy rain. \nGiven the low LCLs, weak low level shear, and subtle backing of the \nlow level wind field in areas, an isolated tornado cannot be \nentirely ruled out but it will not be the primary concern. Limiting \nfactors will be if instability ends up being much less than progged \nand/or if convection well to our northwest generates a significant \ncold pool and ruins the convective potential for most of northern \nlower MI as the boundary is forced eastward quicker. Nevertheless, \nthe signal is there within the suite of hi-res guidance for heavy \nrain in spots. Localized flooding not out of the question if the \ningredients come together just right. Activity diminishes through \nthe night with the loss of daytime heating and the main forcing \nmoving eastward.\n\nTroughing continues to dig down across Ontario late week, impinging \nlower heights across the Great Lakes Region. Consequently, cooler \ntemperatures are anticipated Thursday into Friday, although close to \nseasonable for this time of year. Cannot rule out some light showers \nas well during this time frame and mildly breezy winds.  \n\nHeading into this weekend and early next week, ridging builds across \nthe South to Southeast, troughing moves west to east across the N \nPlains and vicinity, and a more overall zonal structure takes hold \nfor N MI. Consequently, low pressure system to the west will advect \nmoisture eastward into the Midwest, with rounds of rainfall \npossible. Most likely timing for rain and storms will be Sunday into \nMonday as moisture advection coincides with upper support. Plenty of \nuncertainty in regards to the details though. High temperatures seem \nto hold steady in the upper 70s to low 80s outside of the typical \nday to day variability given storms and cloud cover.",
